WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.415
In this example, I'll
demonstrate how to delete

2
00:00:02.415 --> 00:00:04.995
a single record from a
table in a database.

3
00:00:04.995 --> 00:00:07.830
I'm using the student table
from a college database and

4
00:00:07.830 --> 00:00:09.330
deleting the record of a student

5
00:00:09.330 --> 00:00:11.075
with the last name of Miller.

6
00:00:11.075 --> 00:00:14.880
I go to the SQL
tab in phpMyAdmin.

7
00:00:15.080 --> 00:00:17.475
I write a delete statement

8
00:00:17.475 --> 00:00:21.150
beginning with the
keyword delete from.

9
00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:25.875
Then I specify the table
name as student table.

10
00:00:25.875 --> 00:00:28.230
I add a where clause and

11
00:00:28.230 --> 00:00:30.345
the condition to delete
the data I want.

12
00:00:30.345 --> 00:00:33.210
I need a database to scan
the list of students and

13
00:00:33.210 --> 00:00:36.285
identify the last name
or value of Miller,

14
00:00:36.285 --> 00:00:38.715
and then delete that
record from the table.

15
00:00:38.715 --> 00:00:48.270
I type where followed by
last name equal to Miller.

16
00:00:48.590 --> 00:00:52.920
I then run this statement
by pressing the go button.

17
00:00:55.090 --> 00:00:57.170
I get a confirmation that

18
00:00:57.170 --> 00:01:00.575
the record Miller has been
deleted from the database.

19
00:01:00.575 --> 00:01:03.650
I can then access
the student table on

20
00:01:03.650 --> 00:01:05.390
the left panel to ensure that

21
00:01:05.390 --> 00:01:08.345
the record or instance of
Miller has been removed.

22
00:01:08.345 --> 00:01:11.135
Let's explore another
example this time

23
00:01:11.135 --> 00:01:13.940
by deleting multiple records
from the student table.

24
00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:15.800
Now I want to delete
the records for

25
00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:18.530
the two students within the
engineering department.

26
00:01:18.530 --> 00:01:20.480
The beginning of the
statement is the

27
00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:23.180
same as the previous example.

28
00:01:24.890 --> 00:01:27.840
I begin with the delete and form

29
00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:29.560
keywords followed by the name

30
00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:30.700
of the table I'm working with,

31
00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:32.470
which is student table.

32
00:01:32.470 --> 00:01:35.935
The where clause is the key
difference in this example.

33
00:01:35.935 --> 00:01:39.035
I type where followed by

34
00:01:39.035 --> 00:01:44.770
department equal.
To engineering.

35
00:01:45.050 --> 00:01:47.410
This instructs SQL to

36
00:01:47.410 --> 00:01:49.930
identify all records
that have a value of

37
00:01:49.930 --> 00:01:52.225
engineering within
the department column

38
00:01:52.225 --> 00:01:54.580
and remove those
rows from the table.

39
00:01:54.580 --> 00:01:56.410
But I need to be careful.

40
00:01:56.410 --> 00:01:59.005
If I don't correctly
specify the where clause,

41
00:01:59.005 --> 00:02:01.955
then all the records in
the table will be deleted.

42
00:02:01.955 --> 00:02:04.165
Now that I've completed
my statement,

43
00:02:04.165 --> 00:02:06.470
I select Go to run it.

44
00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:12.830
I then check the table
by clicking it on

45
00:02:12.830 --> 00:02:16.790
the left panel and

46
00:02:16.790 --> 00:02:18.080
confirm that the records for

47
00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:19.265
the two engineering students

48
00:02:19.265 --> 00:02:21.470
have been deleted
from the table.

49
00:02:21.470 --> 00:02:24.350
Finally, let's quickly explore

50
00:02:24.350 --> 00:02:26.630
how to delete all
records from a table.

51
00:02:26.630 --> 00:02:29.105
In this task, the syntax remains

52
00:02:29.105 --> 00:02:31.790
largely the same as in
the previous examples.

53
00:02:31.790 --> 00:02:33.650
The key difference
is that I removed

54
00:02:33.650 --> 00:02:36.275
the where clause so
that now by syntax,

55
00:02:36.275 --> 00:02:40.935
just state, delete
from student table.

56
00:02:40.935 --> 00:02:44.320
I remove the where clause.

57
00:02:44.870 --> 00:02:50.400
That now my syntax just states
delete from student table.

58
00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:53.180
In other words, I'm
instructing the database

59
00:02:53.180 --> 00:02:55.930
to remove all records
from the student table.

60
00:02:55.930 --> 00:03:00.970
Then I click Go and
confirm the deletion.

61
00:03:01.660 --> 00:03:04.475
Once the deletion
has been confirmed,

62
00:03:04.475 --> 00:03:07.020
I check the student table,

63
00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:10.035
which is now empty.

64
00:03:10.035 --> 00:03:14.040
I now know that all
records have been deleted.